Testing the ways a policy's 'proto' and 'chain' options go wrong.

'proto' is a PORT QUALIFIER: it only ever reaches a rule as a prefix on
sport/dport (files/lib/pbr/pbr.uc), and pbr's own defaulting says as much --
with no proto set it becomes 'tcp udp' when a port is present and 'all' when it
is not. Two consequences, both silent before this change:

  1. proto set, no ports        -> the proto is dropped and the policy routes
                                   EVERY protocol, not the one asked for.
  2. a protocol with no ports   -> emits e.g. 'icmp dport { 53 }', which nft
     (icmp, gre, esp, ...)         refuses. The whole file is rejected, so pbr
     plus a port                   fails to start and NOTHING routes.

Case 2 must therefore reject the policy rather than warn: by the time the
ruleset is refused a warning helps nobody. Case 1 is a warning, and it is
actionable -- for a protocol that does have ports, '0-65535' matches all of it.

The LuCI dropdown is built from /etc/protocols (46 entries on stock 25.12) and
only tcp/udp/sctp/dccp/udplite can carry a port, so 41 of its choices land in
one of these two cases.

'chain' fails the same way as case 2. pbr creates pbr_dstnat plus the chains in
chains_list -- prerouting, forward and output -- and nothing else, so a policy
naming any other chain emits a rule into a chain that does not exist. nft
answers "No such file or directory" and, again, refuses the whole file. The
README documented 'input' and 'postrouting' for years and neither has existed
since before 1.2.2, so this was reachable by following the documentation; a
typo does it too. LuCI offers only the three, but UCI takes any string.

'dstnat' is not in chains_list yet IS created -- files/lib/pbr/nft.uc creates
['dstnat', ...split(chains_list, ' ')] -- and a policy naming it works today,
so the check is against the chains that actually exist rather than against the
documented three -- otherwise a working config would start failing.

